        "description": "<img src='https://i.cbc.ca/ais/6d82a5c8-ac98-48da-81a8-d1c6be1f99e1,1787153148478/full/max/0/default.jpg?im=Crop%2Crect%3D%280%2C206%2C3898%2C2192%29%3BResize%3D%28620%29' alt='A picture of two women holding up a 4x6 photo of themselves as teenagers' width='620' height='349' title='Meena Geltink, 43, and Minal Tijssen, 44, were adopted separately as babies and raised near each other in the Netherlands.'/><p>More than four decades ago, somewhere amid the bustling streets of Mumbai, India, two baby girls were born.\nAdopted by different families in the Netherlands, Meena Geltink and Minal Tijssen grew up mere hours away from each other.\nBut their paths never crossed until one day in 1996, when as teenagers, they happened to attend an event organized for adoptees in the Netherlands.</p>",
